---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import CaseSection from '../../components/CaseSection.astro';

const next = {
  href: '/work/camera-calibration/',
  category: 'Perception tooling',
  title: 'Camera calibration toolkit',
  image: '/images/calibration-target.webp',
  alt: 'Checkerboard calibration target with coordinate axes',
};
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Point-cloud alignment"
  description="Reverse-engineering a custom 3D viewer's coordinate system to align three point-cloud sets, including the reflections a quaternion cannot represent."
  index="03"
  category="3D geometry"
  subtitle="Three point-cloud sets, one viewer with an undocumented coordinate convention, and a transformation chain that had to survive reflections, pivots, and a trajectory file."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Geometry · implementation' },
    { label: 'Timeline', value: '2026' },
    { label: 'Type', value: '3D reconstruction / tooling' },
    { label: 'Stack', value: 'Python · Open3D · NumPy' },
  ]}
  next={next}
>
  <Fragment slot="media">
    <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#problem">01 / Problem</a>
      <a href="#reverse">02 / Reverse-engineering</a>
      <a href="#transform">03 / The transform</a>
      <a href="#result">04 / Result</a>
      <a href="#lessons">05 / Lessons</a>
    </nav>

    <figure class="case-lead-media">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img
          src="/images/pcd-card.webp"
          alt="Aligned point cloud inside a custom viewer, with a raw misaligned inset and coordinate axes"
          width="1200"
          height="900"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The corrected scene in the target viewer. The inset is the same data before the coordinate transform — mirrored,
        inverted, and unusable for navigation.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </Fragment>

  <CaseSection number="01" title="The geometry was correct. The coordinate system was not." id="problem">
    <p class="case-lead">
      Three point-cloud sets arrived with world-space camera poses. The target viewer expected its own native frame.
      Nothing documented what that frame was.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loading the raw data in a standard tool produced a scene that was recognisably a room, but the viewer showed it
      mirrored, upside down, or drifting depending on which correction was applied. The camera trajectory had the same
      problem: it was mathematically consistent with the point clouds, and consistently wrong in the viewer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The task was to compute the exact transform from each source frame into the viewer's frame, apply it to the
      geometry, and make the result verifiable in both Open3D and the custom engine. “Looks approximately right” was not
      good enough; the scene had to be geometrically consistent.
    </p>
    <div class="case-callout">
      The bug was not in the point clouds. It was in the unstated contract between three coordinate conventions: the
      sensor, the world, and the viewer.
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="02" title="Find the convention before writing the transform." id="reverse">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The first useful move was to stop guessing. A dummy XYZ frame, rendered with an identity pose, turns an
      undocumented viewer into a measurable coordinate system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loading the raw clouds and the camera-to-world poses into Open3D established a baseline: the data was internally
      consistent, but its orientation did not match the target. To identify the viewer's axes, I generated a dummy
      coordinate frame in Open3D and loaded it into the viewer with an identity matrix. The result showed the target
      convention: <strong>right-handed, Y-down, Z-outward</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="case-pair">
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/pcd-o3d-before.webp" alt="Dummy XYZ axes in Open3D" width="790" height="726" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>The probe frame in Open3D. X red, Y green, Z blue.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/pcd-o3d-after.webp" alt="The same dummy axes rendered in the custom viewer" width="1071" height="462" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>The same frame in the target viewer. The axes are not where a standard camera convention would put them.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p>
      With the target convention known, the correction decomposed into three pieces: a local sensor alignment, the
      world-space camera path, and a final viewer orientation. The hard part was that the correction included
      <strong>reflections</strong>, not just rotations.
    </p>
    <p>
      That matters because quaternions represent rotations, not mirrors. A reflection matrix has a negative
      determinant; decomposing it into a quaternion and a translation silently loses the handedness. The scene looks
      almost right, then drifts or mirrors somewhere else. The fix was to stop forcing the whole transform through a
      quaternion decomposition and apply the compound matrix directly.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="03" title="Bake the transform into the geometry." id="transform">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The final pipeline applies the full compound equation to every vertex and writes an identity trajectory. The
      viewer has nothing left to reinterpret.
    </p>

    <div class="case-code">
      <div class="case-code__bar"><span>transform.py / the compound equation</span><span>Python</span></div>
      <pre><code><span class="tok-com"># local sensor alignment → world-space camera path → viewer orientation</span>
T_sensor = local_alignment(sensor_frame)
T_world  = camera_to_world(poses)
T_viewer = viewer_orientation(<span class="tok-str">"right-handed"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"y-down"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"z-outward"</span>)

T = T_viewer @ T_world @ T_sensor

<span class="tok-com"># bake the transform into the points; do not ask the viewer to re-apply it</span>
points_viewer = (T[:<span class="tok-str">3</span>, :<span class="tok-str">3</span>] @ points.T).T + T[:<span class="tok-str">3</span>, <span class="tok-str">3</span>]
write_ply(points_viewer, <span class="tok-str">"output_data/viewer_corrected/scene.ply"</span>)

<span class="tok-com"># the trajectory is already expressed in the final frame</span>
write_trajectory(identity_matrices(len(poses)), <span class="tok-str">"output_data/viewer_corrected/traj.txt"</span>)</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
      The identity trajectory is deliberate. Earlier versions let the viewer apply its own transform on top of the
      corrected points, which introduced a second, hidden correction. Baking the geometry into absolute world positions
      and neutralising the trajectory removed that class of bug entirely.
    </p>
    <p>
      This also bypassed a subtler issue: the viewer's rotation pivot was not the world origin. A correct rotation around
      the wrong centroid produces localised drift that looks like a bad point cloud. Absolute positioning avoids the
      pivot question altogether.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="04" title="Before and after, in the same viewport." id="result">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The comparison below is the Open3D view of the raw data and the corrected data. Drag the handle to move between
      them.
    </p>

    <div class="ba" data-ba style="--pos: 50%">
      <div class="ba__frame">
        <img class="ba__img ba__img--before" src="/images/pcd-ba-before.webp" alt="Point cloud in Open3D before alignment: a collapsed, rotated mass" width="1200" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <img class="ba__img" src="/images/pcd-ba-after.webp" alt="Point cloud in Open3D after alignment: a coherent indoor scene" width="1200" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <span class="ba__tag ba__tag--before">Raw</span>
        <span class="ba__tag ba__tag--after">Aligned</span>
        <span class="ba__line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <input class="ba__range" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" aria-label="Compare raw and aligned point clouds" data-ba-range />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="ba__hint">Drag to compare · raw → aligned</p>

    <div class="case-pair">
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/pcd-before.webp" alt="Raw point cloud in the custom viewer, mirrored and inverted" width="491" height="393" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Raw data in the custom viewer. The scene is mirrored and inverted relative to the camera path.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/pcd-after.webp" alt="Corrected point cloud in the custom viewer, showing a coherent indoor scene" width="1192" height="449" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Corrected data in the same viewer. The room, furniture, and camera path now agree.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="case-note">
      The corrected scene is also exported back into the standard Open3D viewer, so the result can be checked outside
      the target engine. Verification is part of the pipeline, not a manual afterthought.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="05" title="What this changed about how I debug geometry." id="lessons">
    <p class="case-lead">
      Most 3D bugs are convention bugs wearing a disguise. The work made that concrete.
    </p>

    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Probe the target, don't infer it.</strong> A dummy axis object with an identity pose is cheaper than an afternoon of guessing.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the determinant.</strong> If a transform includes a reflection, a quaternion decomposition is the wrong representation.</li>
      <li><strong>Bake once, at the boundary.</strong> Apply the compound transform to the geometry and neutralise downstream transforms; hidden layers compound errors.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify in two viewers.</strong> Open3D is the sanity check. The custom viewer is the actual contract.</li>
      <li><strong>Separate pivot from pose.</strong> A correct rotation around the wrong centroid is still wrong.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="case-links">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://github.com/nomadka/pcd_transformation" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        Repository
        <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M2.5 9.5 9.5 2.5M4 2.5h5.5V8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
        </svg>
      </a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/work/camera-calibration/">Next: calibration</a>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseLayout>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-ba]').forEach((widget) => {
    const range = widget.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-ba-range]');
    if (!range) return;
    const update = () => widget.style.setProperty('--pos', `${range.value}%`);
    range.addEventListener('input', update);
    update();
  });
</script>
